---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Our philosophy — security belongs to everyone"
  description="Why FerXium is free forever, why it is built in Rust, and how an open-source antivirus can put privacy, transparency, and user control first."
>
  <section class="page-hero container">
    <span class="eyebrow cyan">A SIMPLE IDEA. A LASTING COMMITMENT.</span>
    <h1>
      Security should be a right.
      <br />
      <span>Not a subscription.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      We’re building FerXium around that belief.
      <br />
      Open code. Honest coverage. You in control.
    </p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose about-prose">
    <section>
      <span class="feature-icon">
        <Icon name="heart" size={25} />
      </span>
      <h2>Why free, forever?</h2>
      <p>
        Protecting a personal computer shouldn’t depend on a monthly bill. FerXium’s source is MIT
        licensed. Every implemented feature belongs to the same free project. There are no premium
        tiers, upgrade prompts, paid scanning quotas, or hidden trials.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open-source licensing makes that promise tangible: you can inspect, modify, and distribute
        the code under its license, regardless of the project’s future.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <span class="feature-icon">
        <Icon name="code" size={25} />
      </span>
      <h2>Why Rust?</h2>
      <p>
        Security tools spend their lives reading untrusted inputs. Rust helps prevent many memory
        safety mistakes in application code, while supporting predictable performance and clear
        ownership boundaries. It doesn’t make an antivirus invulnerable; careful design, testing,
        audits, and a strong threat corpus still matter.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="privacy">
      <span class="feature-icon">
        <Icon name="lock" size={25} />
      </span>
      <h2>Privacy is the starting point.</h2>
      <p>
        The application has no analytics SDKs, telemetry, advertising, or sample uploads. Reports
        and quarantine content stay in private per-user storage. Network traffic counters remain
        local. Cloud lookup is unavailable and disabled in this release.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you configure an update feed, the service contacts that HTTPS host to retrieve signed
        signatures. The host sees the request and your IP address. It does not receive scanned file
        paths, hashes, or contents. This website serves local fonts and assets and includes no
        analytics scripts.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="open-source">
      <span class="feature-icon">
        <Icon name="github" size={25} />
      </span>
      <h2>Transparency over promises.</h2>
      <p>
        The tagline is a statement of ambition. No security product can guarantee unbreakable
        protection. This first source release implements real scanning and monitoring, with example
        signatures and documented limitations. We’ll earn trust through clear release notes,
        independent review, and measured improvements.
      </p>
      <p>
        The complete source is public. Native preview builds include the desktop and protection
        service, with checksums for each download. Publisher signing, independent audits, and a
        vetted signature corpus remain release milestones.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <CTA />
</Layout>
